#
# Supersedes v1. If v1 is already in, replace the same three regions again.
#
# What changed and why v1 still failed:
#
#  * v1 matched prompts with anchored regexes ('(?m)^([\w.-]+)>\s*$'). A real
#    console stream breaks those constantly — a lone \r instead of \r\n, a
#    trailing NUL, an ANSI sequence, and the anchor no longer matches even
#    though the prompt is plainly there. Verified: "\rwkgfw> " and
#    "\r\nwkgfw> \x00" both fail the v1 pattern.
#    v2 normalises the stream (ANSI stripped, \r\n and \r both folded to \n,
#    NUL/backspace removed) and then just looks at the LAST NON-EMPTY LINE and
#    what it ends with. No anchors, nothing to break.
#
#  * v1 sent one enable password. If these two firewalls have NO enable
#    password set, the ASA still prints "Password:" and accepts a BLANK line —
#    sending Cisco123 there gets "Access denied" and it never reaches '#'.
#    v2 tries each candidate in turn: self.secret, $ASA_SECRET,
#    $ASA_KNOWN_SECRET, then blank.
#
#  * v1 gave up if the first CR produced nothing. v2 pokes up to three times.
#
#  * New: set ASA_CONSOLE_DEBUG=1 to dump the raw bytes of every exchange, so
#    a failure shows exactly what the device sent.
